near equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=3 \\\\ -3x-(k+1)y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186425949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db005b4e-86cd-5194-b2fd-10ca4ee59c03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425949 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y &=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186425950 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425950 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c3254d8-7342-5cba-aa85-c696386c710a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425950 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b9e4957-843e-5d56-8704-c60e6e8f032c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425951 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425952 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425952 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9dff08f-68e1-5fe8-9586